// =============================================================================
// Content for the "Vibration modes" page: molecules and their named modes.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This file is JSONC (JSON with Comments). The build step strips // and /* */
// comments before shipping clean JSON to the browser.
//
// IMPORTANT — keep this preamble in sync: whenever a key below is added,
// removed, renamed, or its meaning changes (in schema.py, loader.py, or this
// file), update this comment block in the same change. See CLAUDE.md.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cross-file links — the keys that actually connect this file to bands.jsonc
// and references.bib. Everything else below is this file's own schema.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   band_groups        → must be real Group keys from bands.jsonc's "groups"
//                         table (the same keys bands.jsonc's own `group`
//                         field uses) — not validated against any one band.
//   modes[].band_reference → each entry is a Band `id` or `branch_group` from
//                         bands.jsonc. Validated in validate_vibrations
//                         (src/ir_bands/loader.py) to actually resolve, AND
//                         to share the mode's own `category` — but NOT its
//                         `subtype`, since degenerate sub-modes (e.g.
//                         bend/scissoring + bend/wagging) can legitimately
//                         point at the same observed band.
//   modes[].reference  → citekeys, resolved against references.bib.
//   modes[].atoms      → mirrors the same field on the real bands a mode
//                         points to, and uses bands.jsonc's exact same atoms
//                         vocabulary (e.g. "O=C=O", "O-C-O") so the page
//                         colors it identically to the band chart.
//
// Pixel geometry/displacement vectors for the animated diagrams live in the
// frontend (frontend/src/lib/moleculeGeometry.ts), keyed by molecule.id and
// mode.id — NOT in this file, which is editorial content only.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Top-level shape: { "molecules": [ Molecule, ... ] } — one entry per
// molecule, each carrying its own self-contained list of modes. A mode's id
// only needs to be unique within its molecule's `modes` array, but in
// practice every id in this file is globally unique (prefixed with the
// molecule id) to make band_reference/reference debugging easier.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Molecule fields:
//   id          — unique snake_case, matches the key used in
//                 moleculeGeometry.ts's MOLECULE_GEOMETRY table.
//   label       — SHORT, common display name, e.g. "Formate", "Carbonate".
//                 This is what's shown prominently in the UI: the molecule
//                 selector's pill buttons and the big title at the top of
//                 the molecule card. Optimized for being compact/readable at
//                 a glance, not for chemical precision.
//   species     — LONG form: the actual chemical formula, e.g. "HCOO*",
//                 "CO₃²⁻" (the asterisk marks a surface-bound species, same
//                 convention as bands.jsonc). More explicit than `label`,
//                 used in the one-line subtitle under a mode's title. For
//                 small molecules whose formula is already maximally short
//                 (CO, CO₂), label and species are identical — there's no
//                 separate "common name" worth showing.
//                 Neither label nor species is used to resolve any
//                 cross-reference — they're both purely descriptive. (Note
//                 species here is NOT required to equal bands.jsonc's own
//                 `species` field for the same chemical — that one follows
//                 its own short-common-name convention, e.g. "Formate*".)
//   shape       — "linear" | "nonlinear", the textbook geometry
//                 classification. Combined with the atom count from this
//                 molecule's own entry in moleculeGeometry.ts, the frontend
//                 computes the standard 3N-5 (linear) / 3N-6 (non-linear)
//                 fundamental-mode count and shows how many of those are
//                 actually listed below (e.g. "2 fundamental modes
//                 missing") — an honest, self-updating completeness signal,
//                 not something to edit by hand.
//   band_groups — see "Cross-file links" above.
//   modes       — list of VibrationMode (below).
//
// VibrationMode fields:
//   id          — unique (see note above); matches a key in this molecule's
//                 entry in moleculeGeometry.ts's `modes` map.
//   category, subtype — same enums as bands.jsonc's vibration.category/
//                 subtype (stretch|bend|combination|lattice and
//                 symmetric|asymmetric|scissoring|rocking|wagging|twisting).
//                 Drives the vibration-color chip and the chart isolate-link
//                 (see modes[].band_reference above).
//   label       — this MODE's own short notation label, e.g. "νₐₛ(OCO)" —
//                 distinct from the MOLECULE's label above (the page shows
//                 both: molecule label as the card title, mode label as
//                 each row's title). Follows the localized ν/δ/ρ/ω/τ/γ
//                 notation explained in the page's own in-app "Notation"
//                 box, not the normal-mode-numbered (ν₁, ν₂, ν₃…) form.
//   note        — one or two sentences: what is physically happening (which
//                 atoms move, how — not just a restatement of category/
//                 subtype), and, where one exists, a cross-reference to this
//                 mode's name in the OTHER notation system (the normal-mode-
//                 numbered ν₁/ν₂/ν₃… convention this page deliberately
//                 doesn't use as the primary label — see the in-app
//                 "Notation" box). E.g. "Both C=O bonds stretch in phase
//                 while C stays put — the totally symmetric stretch,
//                 conventionally ν₁."
//   ir_active, raman_active — true | false | null (unknown/undocumented).
//                 When true, the loader auto-adds "ir-active"/"raman-active"
//                 to this mode's `tags` (see below) — don't add those tags
//                 by hand, and don't bother adding the false case as a tag
//                 either (an "inactive" mode isn't a notable fact worth
//                 flagging at a glance; the full active/inactive state is
//                 always shown once the mode is opened).
//   atoms       — see "Cross-file links" above.
//   tags        — free-form, mirrors the real bands' tags vocabulary;
//                 "ir-active"/"raman-active" are auto-added by the loader,
//                 don't write them here.
//   band_reference, reference — see "Cross-file links" above.
// =============================================================================
{
  "molecules": [
    {
      "id": "co2",
      "label": "CO₂",
      "species": "CO₂",
      "shape": "linear",
      "band_groups": ["co2"],
      "modes": [
        {
          "id": "co2_stretch_symmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "symmetric",
          "label": "νₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "Both C=O bonds stretch in phase while C stays put — the totally symmetric stretch, conventionally ν₁. Centrosymmetric, so by the mutual exclusion rule it's IR-inactive but Raman-active.",
          "ir_active": false,
          "raman_active": true,
          "atoms": "O=C=O",
          "tags": ["gas-phase"],
          "band_reference": ["co2_sym_1388"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "co2_stretch_asymmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "asymmetric",
          "label": "νₐₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "One C=O bond lengthens as the other shortens, in antiphase — the asymmetric stretch, conventionally ν₃. Breaks the molecule's center of symmetry, making it IR-active (and, by the mutual exclusion rule, Raman-inactive).",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": false,
          "atoms": "O=C=O",
          "tags": ["gas-phase"],
          "band_reference": ["co2_nu3_branches"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "co2_bend",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "scissoring",
          "label": "δ(OCO)",
          "note": "Both O's swing the same way in-plane while C recoils oppositely, closing and opening the O-C-O angle — the in-plane component of the doubly-degenerate bend, conventionally ν₂. Degenerate with the out-of-plane wagging component below; together the pair is why ν₂ is IR-active.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": false,
          "atoms": "O=C=O",
          "tags": ["gas-phase", "degenerated"],
          "band_reference": ["co2_nu2_branches"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "co2_bend_wagging",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "wagging",
          "label": "ω(OCO)",
          "note": "The same ν₂ bending motion as above, but out of the molecular plane — shown here as the atoms pulsing in size rather than a real 2D displacement, since a flat diagram can't otherwise depict out-of-plane motion. Frequency-coincident with the in-plane scissoring component, so it has no separately resolved band of its own.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": false,
          "atoms": "O=C=O",
          "tags": ["gas-phase", "degenerated"],
          "band_reference": ["co2_nu2_branches"],
          "reference": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "co",
      "label": "CO",
      "species": "CO",
      "shape": "linear",
      "band_groups": ["co"],
      "modes": [
        {
          "id": "co_stretch",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": null,
          "label": "ν(CO)",
          "note": "C and O move apart and together along the single bond — the only vibration a two-atom molecule can have, so there's no symmetric/asymmetric pair and nothing to number ν₁/ν₂/ν₃-style.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "atoms": "C-O",
          "tags": ["gas-phase"],
          "band_reference": ["co_nu1_branches"],
          "reference": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "formate",
      "label": "Formate",
      "species": "HCOO*",
      "shape": "nonlinear",
      "band_groups": ["formate"],
      "modes": [
        {
          "id": "formate_ch_stretch",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": null,
          "label": "ν(CH)",
          "note": "The C-H bond stretches — the key HCOO* marker band, since no other common surface species shares this exact C-H environment. In Fermi resonance with the δ(OCH)+νₐₛ(OCO) combination band on some supports (e.g. CeO₂), splitting into a doublet. The free formate ion has its own conventional ν-numbering in the vibrational-spectroscopy literature (this mode is commonly cited as ν₁), though the exact assignment order varies by source more than CO₂'s does.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "C-H",
          "tags": [],
          "band_reference": ["formate_ch_stretch_2885"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "formate_stretch_asymmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "asymmetric",
          "label": "νₐₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "One C-O bond lengthens as the other shortens, in antiphase — often cited as ν₄ of the free formate ion. Independent of surface binding topology: position is support-dependent, so both monodentate and bidentate coordination are listed below.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "band_reference": ["formate_asym_1585"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "formate_stretch_symmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "symmetric",
          "label": "νₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "Both C-O bonds stretch in phase — often cited as ν₂ of the free formate ion. Paired with νₐₛ(OCO) above; the splitting between the two (Δν) is itself diagnostic of binding geometry. Independent of surface binding topology, so both monodentate and bidentate coordination are listed below.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "band_reference": ["formate_sym_1365"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "formate_och_scissoring",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "scissoring",
          "label": "δ(OCH)",
          "note": "The C-H bond rocks relative to the O-C-O frame, changing the O-C-H angle — often cited as ν₃ of the free formate ion. Often overlaps νₛ(OCO) and isn't always separately resolved in a spectrum.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-H",
          "tags": [],
          "band_reference": ["formate_och_bend_1390"],
          "reference": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "carbonate",
      "label": "Carbonate",
      "species": "CO₃²⁻",
      "shape": "nonlinear",
      "band_groups": ["carbonate"],
      "modes": [
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_asymmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "asymmetric",
          "label": "νₐₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "Two of the three C-O bonds stretch oppositely (one lengthens, one shortens) while the third stays put — one component of the doubly-degenerate asymmetric stretch, ν₃ of the free CO₃²⁻ ion (D₃h symmetry). Independent of surface binding topology — listed for both monodentate (smaller splitting from νₛ) and bidentate (larger splitting) coordination, since the splitting itself is diagnostic of which one it is.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "band_reference": ["carbonate_monodentate_asym_1500", "carbonate_bidentate_asym_1645"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_symmetric",
          "category": "stretch",
          "subtype": "symmetric",
          "label": "νₛ(OCO)",
          "note": "All three C-O bonds stretch in phase — the totally symmetric stretch, ν₁ of the free ion. For the free, symmetric CO₃²⁻ ion this is IR-inactive by symmetry; it becomes IR-active here because surface binding (mono-/bidentate) lowers that symmetry. Independent of surface binding topology — listed for both monodentate and bidentate coordination.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "band_reference": ["carbonate_monodentate_sym_1400", "carbonate_bidentate_sym_1300"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_scissoring",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "scissoring",
          "label": "δ(OCO)",
          "note": "Two of the three O's swing toward/apart in-plane while the third stays fixed — the in-plane bend, ν₄ of the free ion (doubly degenerate there; surface binding can resolve the degeneracy). Generic surface carbonate, not specific to a binding mode.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": [],
          "band_reference": ["carbonate_scissoring_680"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_wagging",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "wagging",
          "label": "γ(OCO)",
          "note": "The central C moves out of the O-C-O plane while the O's stay put — the out-of-plane bend, ν₂ of the free ion.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": null,
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "tags": [],
          "band_reference": ["carbonate_wagging_875"],
          "reference": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
